      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's simple distillation. Heat salt water in a flask. Water molecules escape as vapour, which just means a substance in its gas state, but the salt ions don't. Salt is non-volatile, so it doesn't evaporate at these temperatures. The vapour flows into the condenser, a tube with cold water running through an outer jacket. The cold glass takes energy from the vapour, so it condenses back into liquid water. That liquid is called the distillate, and it drips into the receiver. The salt stays behind in the flask.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "surprise-two-liquids",
      "audio": "public/audio/chem-y11-m1-v04/surprise-two-liquids.wav",
