        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Now try two liquids. Ethanol boils at seventy eight degrees, and water boils at a hundred. You might expect the ethanol to boil off at seventy eight, and leave the water behind. That's not what happens. A mixture that's seventeen percent ethanol by mass starts boiling at about eighty nine degrees, and both kinds of molecule escape. Ethanol molecules are held less strongly by their neighbours, so more of them get away. The vapour is richer in ethanol, about sixty two percent, but that's still a lot of water.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
