          "word": "a",
          "start": 23.49100000000007,
          "end": 23.53100000000007,
          "score": 0.496
        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "But here's the limit. However tall the column, the distillate never gets past about ninety six percent ethanol by mass. At that composition, the vapour has exactly the same composition as the liquid, so another cycle changes nothing. A mixture like this, which boils without changing its composition, is called an azeotrope. It boils at one steady temperature, just like a pure substance. So a steady thermometer reading is a hint, not proof of purity.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
