      "pronunciationText": "That's what a fractionating column does, automatically. It's a tall tube packed with glass beads, between the flask and the condenser. It's hottest at the bottom and cooler towards the top. Rising vapour condenses on the cooler beads, and that liquid trickles down. Hotter vapour rising from below boils some of it again, and more of the ethanol escapes. This happens over and over up the column, so each level holds vapour a little richer in ethanol than the level below. By the top, the vapour is mostly ethanol, at about seventy eight degrees.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
